
[38;5;231m⏺[39m Good. Now the decomposition, since this is still more than one spec's worth of work.

  [1mThree sub-projects, in dependency order:[0m

  1. [1mTasks core[0m — the task model (id, title, due date, status, timestamps), local persistence, CRUD UI, and the
     append-only change log every mutation writes to. Unavoidable prerequisite: there is nothing to notify [3mabout[0m until
     tasks exist. Can be kept deliberately thin.
  2. [1mNotification engine[0m — trigger rules evaluated against tasks and the change log (due-soon, overdue, stale), a
     scheduler that decides [3mwhen[0m to evaluate, and the state that prevents the same alert firing twelve times (dedupe,
     snooze, dismissed, read).
  3. [1mDelivery & inbox UI[0m — the bell with an unread count, the notification list, in-page toasts, and optionally OS-level
     alerts via the Web Notifications API for when the tab isn't focused.

  Each gets its own spec → plan → implementation cycle. The interesting design work — and the part you actually asked
  for — is #2 and #3; #1 is scaffolding sized to support them and no larger.

  One honest caveat about #3: a browser tab that's closed runs no code, so a purely local app cannot reliably fire a
  "your task is due" alert at 9am when nothing is open. Options are a Service Worker with the Notification Triggers API
  (Chromium-only, patchy), notifying only on next open ("2 tasks went overdue while you were away"), or accepting a
  server later. I'd design for [3mnotify-on-next-open[0m as the baseline and treat live OS alerts as a progressive enhancement
  while the tab is open — but flag it now rather than have it surprise you at implementation.
